# State-matched one-pass hashing experiment v1

## Binding and exposure

This prospective development test implements the two endpoints frozen in
`discriminative_state_matched_hash_scope_v1.md`.  The 20 Newsgroups test split
was already inspected by prior compression and hashing development; these
three order seeds are therefore not independent confirmation.

## Hypothesis and falsifier

The earlier 2^18 averaged endpoint may have failed partly because its retained
classifier arrays exceeded the full sparse Ridge coefficient state.  Reducing
the hash dimension at the algebraic state crossover may retain enough quality
while becoming smaller and faster.  The hypothesis is falsified on this
benchmark if neither frozen endpoint meets all three unchanged gates.

## Endpoints and fixed protocol

- `hash_16384_no_average`: 2^14 features, `average=False`.
- `hash_4096_average`: 2^12 features, `average=True`.
- Native 20 Newsgroups train/test split, with headers, footers and quotes
  removed; loader cache reuses the recoverable w002 `.deps` material.
- Stateless `HashingVectorizer(alternate_sign=False, norm="l2",
  stop_words="english")`.
- One sequential `SGDClassifier.partial_fit` pass, log loss, alpha 1e-4, L2,
  optimal learning rate, batches of 256, seeds/orderings 19, 71 and 211.
- CPU only, one process and one thread, 4 GiB process address-space limit.

## Baseline and outcomes

The pinned reference is the previously measured full TF-IDF + sparse Ridge
baseline (`discriminative_reservoir_robustness_reference_v1.json`, SHA-256
`3e5a549a5deb564401970429b629c5a4d550640b48f1103a71d07126132beb2b`):
macro-F1 0.6755475838189995 and median wall time 2.522401476991945 seconds.
The state reference is 20 * 17797 float64 coefficients plus 20 float64
intercepts, 2,847,680 bytes.

For each endpoint record macro-F1, accuracy, end-to-end wall time, component
times, all retained NumPy arrays and cumulative peak RSS.  An endpoint passes
only if its median macro-F1 loss is <=0.05, median wall ratio is <=0.75, and
the maximum measured retained NumPy-array bytes is <=2,847,680.

If neither passes, retire streaming hashing on this benchmark and require a
source-defined larger sparse/streaming task before further application claims.
If one passes, treat it only as a simple baseline for a later online-IDF or
moment-estimation comparison, not as a new algorithmic result.
